INT. RAILWAY SIGNAL TOWER - NIGHT

Levers. Brass gauges. A single desk lamp. Rain on the windows.

SIGNALMAN ARCHIBALD NWANKWO-BELL (50s, cardigan, thermos) hunches over a logbook. Across the room, TRAINEE FLORENCIA ZAVALA (20s, oilskin) tends the lever frame.

Behind them both, an old iron stove ticks. Neither looks at it.

                    ARCHIBALD
          Flo, number nine.

                    FLORENCIA
          Pulled.

                    ARCHIBALD
          Number twelve.

                    FLORENCIA
          Pulled.

The phone rings. Both startle. Archibald lifts the receiver.

                    ARCHIBALD
          Tower. Yes. Yes. All clear on the up line.

He hangs up. She steps back from the levers. He stares at the receiver as if it might ring again.

                    FLORENCIA
          What did they want?

                    ARCHIBALD
          Confirmation.

The stove ticks. A boot scrapes on the stairs outside. Archibald stands. Florencia grips a lever.

The door swings open. A drenched man in a conductor's coat staggers in: CONDUCTOR PERCY DALRYMPLE (60s, grey beard, cap in hand).

                    PERCY
          For God's sake, stop the eleven-fifteen.

                    ARCHIBALD
          The eleven-fifteen isn't due until midnight.

                    PERCY
          It left at nine.

Archibald turns toward the board. Florencia lunges for a lever. He grabs her wrist. They freeze. Far away, a whistle shrieks.
